<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kerameikos</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ceramicus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kerameikos was the potters' quarter of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, lying on both sides of the city wall north-west of the Acropolis, and the site of the city's main cemetery. Its graves, excavated since the nineteenth century, provide a continuous record from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> onwards, including Protogeometric and Geometric pottery, and it held the state burials of the war dead.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kerameikos.jpg" alt="Kerameikos" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George E. Koronaios · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kerameikos_Cemetery_on_July_28,_2019.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Kerameikos lies north-west of the Acropolis and takes in a wide area both inside and outside the ancient city walls, on either side of the Dipylon Gate and by the banks of the Eridanos River. Its name comes from the deme of the Kerameis, the potters, and from the Greek word kéramos for pottery clay, from which the English word ceramic derives. The Inner Kerameikos was the potters' quarter within the city, sited there because of the clay carried down by the Eridanos. The Outer Kerameikos covered the cemetery and the Demosion Sema, the public graveyard just outside the walls, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a> delivered his funeral oration in 431 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area was originally marshland along the Eridanos and was used for burial as early as the 3rd millennium BCE. It became an organised cemetery from about 1200 BCE, and cist graves with offerings from that period have been found. After the Persian sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> in 480 BCE the new city wall of 478 BCE changed the area. At the suggestion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/themistocles">Themistocles</a> the funerary sculptures were built into it, and two large gates were set up: the Sacred Gate and the double-arched Dipylon Gate, with state graves beside it for prominent figures including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleisthenes">Cleisthenes</a>.</p><p>Inside the walls, between the two gates, stood the Pompeion, where the procession of the Panathenaic Festival gathered and the nobility of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> ate the sacrificial meat. The army of the Roman dictator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> razed it and much else near the Sacred Gate in his sack of Athens in 86 BCE, an episode <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> called a bloodbath. A storehouse built on its site in the 2nd century CE was destroyed in the invasion of the Heruli in 267 CE, and a new Festival Gate built about 500 CE was destroyed in raids by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> at the end of the 6th century, after which the Kerameikos fell into obscurity.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The site was rediscovered when a Greek worker dug up a stele in April 1863. Excavations began in 1870 under the Greek Archaeological Society and have continued since 1913 under the German Archaeological Institute at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. Work on the Kerameikos metro station revealed a pit holding 89 individuals, dated by pottery to 430 to 426 BCE and widely linked to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plague-of-athens">Plague of Athens</a>. The Kerameikos Museum, with the largest collection of burial-related objects in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, stands on the site.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kerameikos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Unlike many Mycenaean centres it was not destroyed around 1200 BCE, and in the early Iron Age it remained one of the largest communities in Greece, known for its Protogeometric and Geometric pottery from the cemetery of the <b>Kerameikos</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
